Article 2 This Law shall apply to the following labor disputes between employers and workers within the territory of the People's Republic of China:
(1) disputes arising from the confirmation of labor relationships;
(2) disputes arising from the conclusion, performance, modification, rescission, or termination of labor contracts;
(3) disputes arising from removal, dismissal, resignation, or separation;
(4) disputes arising from working hours, rest and leave, social insurance, welfare benefits, training, and labor protection;
(5) disputes arising from labor remuneration, work-related injury medical expenses, economic compensation, or damages; and
(6) other labor disputes provided by laws and regulations.

Article 20 A labor dispute arbitration commission shall maintain a roster of arbitrators.
An arbitrator shall be impartial and upright and shall meet one of the following conditions:
(1) having served as a judge;
(2) engaged in legal research or teaching and having a professional title at or above the intermediate level;
(3) having legal knowledge and having been engaged in professional work such as human resources management or trade union work for not less than five years; or
(4) having practiced as a lawyer for not less than three years.

Article 33 An arbitrator shall withdraw under any of the following circumstances, and the parties also have the right to apply for withdrawal orally or in writing:
(1) being a party to the case or a close relative of a party or an agent;
(2) having an interest in the case;
(3) having other relations with a party or an agent that may affect the impartiality of the award; or
(4) having a private meeting with a party or an agent, or accepting gifts or entertainment from a party or an agent.
The labor dispute arbitration commission shall promptly make a decision on the application for withdrawal and notify the parties orally or in writing.

Article 49 If an employer has evidence to prove that the arbitration award specified in Article 47 of this Law falls under any of the following circumstances, it may, within thirty days from the date of receipt of the award, apply to the Intermediate People's Court at the place where the labor dispute arbitration commission is located for setting aside the award:
(1) the application of laws or regulations is indeed erroneous;
(2) the labor dispute arbitration commission had no jurisdiction;
(3) the statutory procedures were violated;
(4) the evidence on which the award was based was forged;
(5) the other party concealed evidence sufficient to affect the impartiality of the award; or
(6) the arbitrator engaged in acts of soliciting or accepting bribes, engaging in malpractice for personal gain, or perverting the law in adjudicating the case.
If the People's Court, after forming a collegial panel to examine and verify, finds that the award falls under any of the circumstances provided in the preceding paragraph, it shall rule to set aside the award.
If the arbitration award is set aside by the People's Court, the parties may file a lawsuit with the People's Court regarding the labor dispute matter within fifteen days from the date of receipt of the ruling.
